B.hS=new A.ZM("EMERGENT",0,"emergent")
B.ad1=new A.ir(0,"Sit Patient Upright",B.hS,"Immediately sit the patient upright with legs dangling. This uses orthostatic pooling to lower BP by approximately 10-20 mmHg.","Monitor for symptomatic hypotension if patient has baseline low BP. Support trunk if needed for stability.","Gravity-mediated venous pooling in the lower extremities reduces preload and lowers BP. This is the fastest non-pharmacologic intervention.")
B.ad4=new A.ir(1,"Loosen Tight Clothing & Equipment",B.hS,"Remove abdominal binders, TED hose, tight clothing, leg bag straps, and any constrictive devices below the level of injury.","Check for kinked catheter tubing, tight shoes, restrictive orthotics, or wrinkled sheets under the patient.","Any constrictive stimulus below the lesion can trigger or worsen the sympathetic response. Removing these eliminates potential noxious stimuli.")
B.ad_=new A.ir(2,"Check Blood Pressure",B.hS,"Obtain BP immediately and recheck every 2-5 minutes. SBP rise of 20-40 mmHg above baseline is significant in SCI (baseline often 90-110 mmHg).","SBP elevation >=20-40 mmHg above patient's baseline (SCI baseline is often 90-110 mmHg) meets diagnostic criteria. SBP >180 mmHg is a hypertensive emergency with risk of stroke, seizure, and death.","Objective BP measurement confirms the diagnosis of AD and guides the urgency of pharmacologic intervention.")
B.ad2=new A.ir(3,"Check Bladder (Most Common Cause: 75-85%)",B.hS,"If catheterised: check for kinks, blockage, full bag. Irrigate or replace catheter using lidocaine jelly. If not catheterised: perform straight catheterisation with lidocaine jelly.","Distended bladder on palpation. Catheter draining? Bag full? Tubing kinked or clamped? UTI signs?","Bladder distension is the #1 cause of AD. Draining even 500 mL can resolve the episode. Use lidocaine jelly to prevent further noxious afferent input.")
B.DE=new A.ZM("MONITORING",1,"monitoring")
B.ad5=new A.ir(4,"Check Bowel (Second Most Common Cause)",B.DE,"With BP still elevated after bladder check: apply lidocaine jelly to rectum, wait 2 minutes, then perform gentle digital exam. Remove any impacted stool.","Faecal impaction, rectal distension. If digital stimulation triggers worsening symptoms, STOP and treat pharmacologically first.","Rectal distension/impaction is the second most common trigger. Lidocaine anesthetises the rectum to prevent worsening the sympathetic response during exam.")
B.ad0=new A.ir(5,"Check Skin & Other Causes",B.DE,"Inspect for pressure injuries, ingrown toenails, burns, tight orthotics, DVT, fractures, or any noxious stimulus below the injury level.","Skin breakdown, swelling (DVT/fracture), temperature changes, insect bites, abdominal distension (acute abdomen).","After bladder and bowel, skin and musculoskeletal causes account for most remaining triggers. A systematic head-to-toe survey below the lesion is essential.")
B.ad3=new A.ir(6,"Pharmacologic Treatment",B.hS,"If BP remains elevated (SBP elevation >=20-40 mmHg above patient's baseline; SCI baseline is often 90-110 mmHg) after removing identifiable causes, initiate rapid-acting antihypertensive therapy while continuing to search for the trigger.","Response to medication within 5-10 minutes. Recheck BP every 2-5 minutes. Watch for rebound hypotension after stimulus removal.","Pharmacologic treatment buys time while the noxious stimulus is identified. Use short-acting agents to avoid rebound hypotension once the stimulus is removed.")
