o=A.aV(A.a(["Receptor","Location","Neurotransmitter","Effect","Clinical Application"],b6),c,A.a([A.a(["M3 Muscarinic","Detrusor body","Acetylcholine (PNS)","Detrusor CONTRACTION \u2192 voiding","Block with antimuscarinics (oxybutynin) to reduce overactivity"],b6),A.a(["Alpha-1 adrenergic","Bladder neck, internal sphincter, prostatic urethra",d,"Smooth muscle CONTRACTION \u2192 storage / outlet resistance","Block with alpha blockers (tamsulosin) to reduce outlet resistance"],b6),A.a(["Beta-3 adrenergic","Detrusor body",d,"Detrusor RELAXATION \u2192 storage","Stimulate with beta-3 agonists (mirabegron) to increase capacity"],b6),A.a(["Beta-2 adrenergic","Bladder neck","Norepinephrine","Bladder neck RELAXATION during voiding","Contributes to voiding phase coordination"],b6),A.a(["Nicotinic","Autonomic ganglia","Acetylcholine","Ganglionic transmission","Not commonly targeted pharmacologically"],b6)],p),"Receptor Pharmacology of the Lower Urinary Tract")
n=A.af("Board Pearl: DSD Neuroanatomy","Detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia (DSD) occurs ONLY when the lesion is BETWEEN the pontine micturition center and the sacral micturition center (S2-S4). The PMC normally sends descending signals to RELAX the external sphincter during detrusor contraction. When this pathway is interrupted (suprasacral SCI), the sphincter contracts reflexively during voiding, creating dangerously high intravesical pressures. Up to 85% of patients with suprasacral SCI develop DSD.")
m=A.B(B.ar,A.a(["During filling, sympathetic activity increases (T11-L2): beta-3 receptors relax detrusor, alpha-1 receptors contract bladder neck","Pudendal nerve activity increases progressively as bladder fills (guarding reflex) \u2014 keeps external sphincter contracted","When cortical decision to void occurs: PMC activates parasympathetic outflow, inhibits sympathetic and pudendal activity simultaneously","This coordinated switch from storage to voiding is lost in suprasacral SCI \u2192 DSD"],b6),B.ac,"Guarding Reflex & Storage")
